{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "Oregon’s groundbreaking initiative to decriminalize drug possession will conclude on Sunday, as a revised law takes effect, reinstating penalties for small amounts of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. The new legislation, HB4002, offers those caught with these drugs the option to either face charges or enter a treatment program, which involves completing a mental health regimen and engaging in an intervention initiative to avoid fines. Holding personal-use amounts will now be classified as a misdemeanor, carrying a potential sentence of up to half a year in jail. The law also seeks to strengthen police enforcement against public drug use and imposes stricter punishments for trafficking near sensitive areas such as parks.\n\nThe updated statute encourages, though does not enforce, local governments to establish alternatives to prosecution, steering individuals away from legal consequences and toward addiction and mental health support. Advocates argue this approach allows regions to tailor programs to their available resources, but critics warn it could lead to inconsistent and unfair policies across different areas. “The requirements for intervention are quite restrictive: no additional offenses, no outstanding warrants, no aggressive conduct, and stable health,” noted Portland’s law enforcement leader, Bob Day.\n\nIf someone qualifies for intervention, officers will notify the local intervention coordination line to verify eligibility and link them to a mobile mental health response team, which must arrive within 30 minutes. “If the mental health responders aren’t available within that window, we’ll proceed with detainment,” Day explained. “We can’t delay indefinitely.”\n\nThe rollback reflects shifting attitudes toward drug policy, balancing enforcement with calls for compassionate, resource-conscious solutions.\n\n***\nP.P.S ['age', 'country', 'eat', 'shopping', 'tongue']", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.0026003853417932987}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.12765175104141235}, {"token": " ground", "logprob": -0.9779071807861328}, {"token": "breaking", "logprob": -0.0002681849291548133}, {"token": " initiative", "logprob": -0.7025636434555054}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.0022870355751365423}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.1957862228155136}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -5.8412379075889476e-06}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.821448318485636e-06}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -1.375806450843811}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.01649809628725052}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.4226418137550354}, {"token": " conclude", "logprob": -0.16882404685020447}, {"token": " on", "logprob": -0.31305745244026184}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -4.9470632802695036e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.21901057660579681}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -1.1612952947616577}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.007908697240054607}, {"token": " revised", "logprob": -0.6380601525306702}, {"token": " law", "logprob": -1.0932871103286743}, {"token": " takes", "logprob": -1.0234266519546509}, {"token": " effect", "logprob": -0.08069990575313568}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.613957941532135}, {"token": " rein", "logprob": -0.3811814486980438}, {"token": "st", "logprob": -0.0005611990345641971}, {"token": "ating", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.010380562394857407}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -5.793403761344962e-05}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -1.3121203184127808}, {"token": " amounts", "logprob": -0.9757017493247986}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0003014348621945828}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -1.3069270849227905}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.2548908293247223}, {"token": " fent", "logprob": -0.019614193588495255}, 